            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on the three lines.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a **non‑sunny** line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny lines meeting \\(T_{3}\\)\n\nChecking each line of the three types (1) we obtain the following incidences:\n\n| line | points of \\(T_{3}\\) on it |\n|------|---------------------------|\n| \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) |\n| \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) |\n| \\(y=3\\) | \\((1,3)\\) |\n| \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) |\n| \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) |\n| \\(x=3\\) | \\((3,1)\\) |\n| \\(x+y=2\\) | \\((1,1)\\) |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) |\n| \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) |\n\nThus any non‑sunny line that meets \\(T_{3}\\) contains at most three points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Reduction to the remaining points\n\nAssume that three distinct lines cover \\(T_{3}\\) and exactly two of them are sunny.  \nLet \\(L\\) be the unique non‑sunny line; define  \n\n\\[\nR = T_{3}\\setminus L .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|L|\\le 3\\), we have \\(|R|\\ge 3\\).  \nWe shall show that for every possible choice of \\(L\\) the set \\(R\\) cannot be covered by only two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Exhaustion of the possibilities for \\(L\\)\n\n#### **Case A: \\(|L|=3\\).**  \nThe only non‑sunny lines containing three points of \\(T_{3}\\) are  \n\n\\[\nL\\in\\{\\,y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=4\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L:y=1\\):* then \\(R=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nThe slopes of the three possible pairs are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,2)-(1,3)&:\\text{vertical (undefined)},\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,2)&:0,\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,2)&:-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes are non‑sunny, so a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(R\\).  \nSince \\(|R|=3\\), two sunny lines cannot cover all of \\(R\\) – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(L:x=1\\):* then \\(R=\\{(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nThe three pairwise slopes are \\(0\\) (horizontal), undefined (vertical) and \\(-1\\); again each pair lies on a non‑sunny line, so a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(R\\).  Contradiction.\n\n*If \\(L:x+y=4\\):* then \\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe pairwise slopes are undefined, \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); the same argument yields a contradiction.\n\nHence no configuration exists when the non‑sunny line contains three points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### **Case B: \\(|L|=2\\).**  \nThe non‑sunny lines with exactly two points of \\(T_{3}\\) are  \n\n\\[\nL\\in\\{\\,y=2,\\;x=2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L:y=2\\):* then  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,3)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll pairs of points of \\(R\\) are examined:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope} \\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(2,1) & 0 \\\\\n(1,1)-(3,1) & 0 \\\\\n(2,1)-(3,1) & 0 \\\\\n(1,1)-(1,3) & \\text{vertical} \\\\\n(2,1)-(1,3) & -2 \\\\\n(3,1)-(1,3) & -1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nOnly the pair \\((2,1),(1,3)\\) has a sunny slope (\\(-2\\)); every other pair lies on a non‑sunny line.  \nThus a sunny line can contain at most the two points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\).  \nThe remaining points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) are collinear on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), which is non‑sunny, so they cannot be covered by a sunny line.  \nConsequently two sunny lines cannot cover all four points of \\(R\\) – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(L:x=2\\):* the situation is symmetric (swap the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)).  The same reasoning leads to a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### **Case C: \\(|L|=1\\).**  \nThe non‑sunny lines meeting \\(T_{3}\\) in a single point are  \n\n\\[\nL\\in\\{\\,y=3,\\;x=3,\\;x+y=2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L:y=3\\):* then  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\},\n\\qquad |R|=5 .\n\\]\n\nWe claim that any three points of \\(R\\) are collinear only on a non‑sunny line.  \nIndeed, checking the ten triples of \\(R\\) shows that the only collinear triple is \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (slope \\(0\\)).  All other triples are non‑collinear.  \nHence a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(R\\).  \nWith only two sunny lines we could cover at most four points, leaving at least one point of \\(R\\) uncovered – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(L:x=3\\):* the same argument applies after swapping \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\n*If \\(L:x+y=2\\):* then  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\},\n\\qquad |R|=5 .\n\\]\n\nAgain a direct check of all triples shows that any three collinear points lie on a non‑sunny line (either \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) or \\(x+y=4\\)).  Therefore a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(R\\), and two sunny lines cannot cover all five points – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nEvery possible choice for the unique non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction with the requirement that t