\\)).  For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,2m+1\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = x + (i-m-1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of every \\(L_i\\) is \\(1\\) (sunny).  The set of differences \\(b-a\\) taken by points of \\(T_n\\) is exactly the interval \\([-(2m),2m]\\).  The \\(2m+1\\) lines (2) correspond to the \\(2m+1\\) central differences  \n\n   \\[\n   -(m),-(m-1),\\dots ,0,\\dots ,m .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\in[-2m,2m]\\); if \\(|b-a|\\le m\\) it lies on one of the lines (2).  \n   The remaining points are those with \\(|b-a|>m\\); they occur only on the two outer \u201cedges\u2019\u2019 of the triangle and are precisely the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=(a,\\,n+1-a)\\quad\\text{or}\\quad (a,b)=(a,\\,n+2-a)\n   \\]\n\n   with \\(1\\le a\\le m\\).  Each such point belongs to a line \\(L_{i}\\) with \\(i\\) equal to its difference plus \\(m+1\\); thus **every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines**.  Hence for odd \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) is realizable.\n\n   *Lemma 3 (any \\(n\\), any \\(k\\le n-1\\)).*  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   - Choose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) given by  \n\n     \\[\n     S_j:\\; y = x + j\\qquad (j= -\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor,\\dots ,\\lceil k/2\\rceil-1).\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     Each such line contains at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\); we shall use only the first two points on each line, namely the pairs  \n\n     \\[\n     (t,\\,t+j),\\;(t+1,\\,t+1+j)\n     \\]\n\n     with the smallest possible \\(t\\) that keeps both points in \\(T_n\\).  Thus each \\(S_j\\) covers **exactly two distinct points** of \\(T_n\\), and the \\(k\\) lines together cover \\(2k\\) points, all distinct because the chosen pairs have different differences \\(j\\).\n\n   - Remove those \\(2k\\) points from \\(T_n\\).  The remaining set consists of the points whose column index \\(a\\) satisfies  \n\n     \\[\n     a\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     (Indeed, each removed pair uses the smallest possible column, so after removing \\(k\\) pairs the first \\(k\\) columns are exhausted.)  \n\n   - For each remaining column \\(a=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) place the vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     V_a:\\; x=a .\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     The line \\(V_a\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); all of them belong to the reduced set because we have never removed a point with column \\(a\\le n-k\\).  Consequently the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (4) cover **all** points that are left after the sunny lines have been placed.\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, the others are vertical (hence non\u2011sunny).  This construction works for every \\(k\\le n-1\\) and for every parity of \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n   * From Lemma\u202f1 we have the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.  \n   * Lemma\u202f2 shows that for odd \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) is attainable.  \n   * Lemma\u202f3 provides a construction for every admissible \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (both even and odd \\(n\\)).  \n\n   Hence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated in the verdict.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\ns_n:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line then \\(b=ma+b\\).  Solving for \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na=\\frac{b-b}{m}\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nThus the integer points of the line form an arithmetic progression whose step is \\(|m|^{-1}\\) (after clearing denominators).  \nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the projection of the line onto the \\(a\\)-axis has length at most \\(n\\); consequently at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) integer points can belong to the line. \u220e\n\n\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then the total number of (not necessarily distinct) incidences of points of \\(T_n\\) with the lines is at most \\(n s_n\\).  Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be incident to at least one line, we obtain\n\n\\[\nn s_n\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor **even** \\(n\\) we have \\(s_n=n/2\\); (1) becomes \\(n^2/2\\ge n(n+1)/2\\), i.e. \\(n\\ge n+1\\), a contradiction.  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{If }n\\text{ is even, }k=n\\text{ is impossible}.}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor **odd** \\(n\\) we have \\(s_n=(n+1)/2\\) and (1) holds with equality, so the bound does not forbid \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for the extremal case \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nLet \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  Consider the family of lines\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (i-m-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,2m+1 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll have slope \\(1\\), therefore they are sunny.  \nThe set of differences \\(b-a\\) taken by points of \\(T_n\\) is the integer interval \\([-(2m),2m]\\).  \nThe lines (2) correspond to the central \\(2m+1\\) differences  \n\n\\[\n-(m),-(m-1),\\dots ,0,\\dots ,m .\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Proof of claim.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).  \nIf \\(|d|\\le m\\) then \\(d\\) equals one of the numbers \\(-(m),\\dots ,m\\); the line \\(L_{d+m+1}\\) has equation \\(y=x+d\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nIf \\(|d|>m\\) then \\(d\\) equals either \\(-(2m),\\dots ,-(m+1)\\) or \\(m+1,\\dots ,2m\\).  \nIn the first sub\u2011case we have \\(b\\le a-m-1\\); because \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) this forces \\(a\\le m\\) and \\(b\\ge m+2\\).  Such a point is \\((a,\\,n+1-a)\\) and satisfies \\(b-a=-(n+1-2a)\\), which is one of the differences represented by the lines (2) after adding the constant \\(m+1\\).  A symmetric argument works for the second sub\u2011case.  Hence the point also belongs to a line of the family. \u220e\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd, }k=n\\text{ is attainable}.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  We shall exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines that together cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.1  The sunny lines\n\nChoose the integers  \n\n\\[\nj_1,j_2,\\dots ,j_k\\in\\Big\\{-\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor,\\dots ,\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Big\\rceil-1\\Big\\}\n\\]\n\nso that they are distinct.  \nFor each \\(j\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = x + j .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll have slope \\(1\\) (sunny).  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the smallest column index that yields a point of \\(T_n\\) on \\(S_j\\) is  \n\n\\[\nt_j:=\\max\\{1,\\,1-j\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe two points  \n\n\\[\nP_{j,1}=(t_j,\\,t_j+j),\\qquad \nP_{j,2}=(t_j+1,\\,t_j+1+j)\n\\]\n\nboth belong to \\(T_n\\) (because \\(t_j+j\\ge1\\) and \\(2t_j+1+j\\le n+1\\) by the choice of \\(t_j\\)).  \nWe let the line \\(S_j\\) be used **only** for these two points.  \nSince the differences \\(j\\) are distinct, the sets \\(\\{P_{j,1},P_{j,2}\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint; therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover exactly \\(2k\\) distinct points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.2  The remaining points\n\nDelete the \\(2k\\) points just described.  \nObserve that for each \\(j\\) the two removed points occupy the two smallest still\u2011available columns of the triangle; consequently after removing all \\(k\\) pairs the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) are completely exhausted.  \nHence every point that remains has its first coordinate \\(a\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\nk+1\\le a\\le n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor each such column we place the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\; x=a ,\\qquad a=k+1,\\dots ,n .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(V_a\\) contains precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); all of them belong to the reduced set because we never removed a point with column \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  Thus the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5) cover **all** points that are left after the sunny lines have been placed.\n\n#### 3.3  Verification\n\n*Number of lines.*  We have introduced \\(k\\) sunny lines (3) and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5); altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  Every point of \\(T_n\\) is either one of the \\(2k\\) points assigned to a sunny line, or it lies in a column \\(a\\ge k+1\\) and is therefore covered by the corresponding vertical line.  Hence the union of the \\(n\\) lines contains the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Sunny count.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines of type (3) are sunny; the vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\nThus for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have produced a valid configuration. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nCombining the three parts:\n\n* By Lemma\u202f1 and (5) the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\) is even.\n* Lemma\u202f2 (6) shows that the case \\(k=n\\) is possible when \\(n\\) is odd.\n* Lemma\u202f3 provides constructions for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), irrespective of the parity of \\(n\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n,\\; k\\neq n\\text{ if }n\\text{ is even}\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**"
