"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nThe only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound** \u2013 Show that any configuration with at least two sunny lines cannot cover the three \u201ccorner\u2019\u2019 points  \n   \\((1,1),\\;(1,n),\\;(n,1)\\).  \n   *A sunny line can contain at most one of these three points, while a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most two.*  \n   With \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines the total number of incidences with the three corner points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot 1+(n-k)\\cdot 2 = 2n-k < 3\\quad\\text{for }k\\ge 2,\n   \\]\n\n   which is impossible because the three points must all be covered. Hence \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\)** \u2013 Use the \\(n\\) antidiagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   each of which is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on exactly one of these lines, so a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=1\\)** \u2013 Take the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=x+1,\n   \\]\n\n   whose slope is \\(1\\) (hence sunny).  \n   The line \\(L\\) contains all integer points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b-a=1\\); in particular it contains every point of the antidiagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\) (the only antidiagonal that would otherwise be missing).  \n   Use the remaining \\(n-1\\) antidiagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n\n   **except** the line \\(x+y=n+2\\).  \n   Together the \\(n\\) lines cover every point of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly one of them (\\(L\\)) is sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists.\n\nSince we have exhibited constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) and proved that \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).  \n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n),\\qquad P_3=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line that contains two of these points must be one of the following:\n\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (contains \\(P_1,P_2\\));\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (contains \\(P_1,P_3\\));\n* the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (contains \\(P_2,P_3\\)).\n\nAll three are **non\u2011sunny** (they are respectively parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, the \\(x\\)-axis and the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nConsequently a **sunny** line can contain **at most one** of the three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can contain **at most two** of them (the three lines listed above each contain exactly two).  \n\nSuppose a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines and that \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \nThe total number of incidences of the three points with the whole family of lines is then at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot 1+(n-k)\\cdot 2 = 2n-k \\;<\\; 3,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(k\\ge 2\\) implies \\(2n-k\\le 2n-2<3\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nThus the three points cannot all be covered \u2013 a contradiction.  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le 1}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nEvery point