------------|----------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(\\ell_2\\) |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) (since \\(1+3=4\\)) |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_1\\) |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(\\ell_3\\) |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_3\\) |\n\nThus every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nChoose two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;x=1,\n\\]\n\nand one sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_3:\\;y=x\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\]\n\nAll three lines are distinct; \\(\\ell_3\\) is sunny because its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).\n\nCoverage check:\n\n| point | line(s) containing it |\n|-------|------------------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(\\ell_2\\) |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(\\ell_2\\) |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_1\\) |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(\\ell_3\\) |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_1\\) |\n\nHence all six points are covered and exactly one line (\\(\\ell_3\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  \\(k=3\\) (all three lines sunny)\n\nWe must partition \\(T_3\\) into three disjoint pairs, each pair defining a sunny line.  \nThe following three pairs work:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_1&=\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}, &\\text{slope }1,\\\\\nP_2&=\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}, &\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\nP_3&=\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}, &\\text{slope }-2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); therefore the corresponding lines are sunny.  Explicitly,\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach line contains exactly the two points of its pair, the three lines are distinct, and together they cover all six points of \\(T_3\\).  Thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  \\(k=2\\) (exactly two sunny lines)\n\nAssume a configuration with two sunny lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\sigma_2\\) and one non‑sunny line \\(\\nu\\).  \nSince a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_3\\), the three lines together can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n2+2+|\\,\\nu\\cap T_3\\,| \\le 4+|\\,\\nu\\cap T_3\\,|\n\\]\n\npoints.  To cover all six points we need \\(|\\,\\nu\\cap T_3\\,|\\ge2\\); hence \\(\\nu\\) must contain at least two points of \\(T_3\\).\n\nAll non‑sunny lines that contain at least two points of \\(T_3\\) are:\n\n* \\(x=2\\) (contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\));\n* \\(x+y=3\\) (contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\));\n* \\(y=1\\) (contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\));\n* \\(x=1\\) (contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\));\n* \\(x+y=4\\) (contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\)).\n\nWe examine each possibility.\n\n1. **\\(\\nu = y=1\\).**  \n   Remaining points: \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  \n   Any two of these are either vertical, horizontal, or have slope \\(-1\\); consequently no pair can be placed on a sunny line.  Thus at most one of the three points can be covered by a sunny line, leaving at least two uncovered – contradiction.\n\n2. **\\(\\nu = x=1\\).**  \n   Remaining points: \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n   As above, any two of them are vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\); no sunny line can contain two of them.  Hence we cannot cover the three points with only two sunny lines.\n\n3. **\\(\\nu = x+y=4\\).**  \n   Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   Again every pair among these three has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); no sunny line can contain two of them, so two sunny lines cannot cover all three points.\n\n4. **\\(\\nu = x=2\\).**  \n   Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n   The only sunny pair among them is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  After using a sunny line for this pair, 