e  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y=i\\,x+(n+1-i) .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of each \\(s_i\\) is \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in U_k\\) write \\((a,b)=(a',b')+(0,n+1-k)\\) with \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\).  \n   By the property of the construction for \\(T_k\\) there exists a unique \\(i\\) such that \\(b'=i a'+(k+1-i)\\).  \n   Substituting \\(b=b'+n+1-k\\) yields \\(b=i a+(n+1-i)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(s_i\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{s_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) cover all points of \\(U_k\\).\n\n5. **Putting the two parts together.**  \n   The family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonals (2) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines (4) is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of them.  \n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Since the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), and the extreme cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) have already been exhibited, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nd_j:\\;x+y=j+1\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(d_j\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=s\\) for some integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\); then \\((a,b)\\) lies on