            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the following family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\):\n\n* vertical (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n  \\[\n  V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k);\n  \\]\n\n* sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)  \n\n  \\[\n  S_i:\\;y=x-(n-k+i)+1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll \\(V_j\\) are distinct and have slope \\(\\infty\\); all \\(S_i\\) have\nslope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence are sunny, and they are distinct\nbecause their intercepts \\(-(n-k+i)+1\\) are different.\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\); consequently it lies on\nthe vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a>n-k\\).  \nPut \\(p:=a-(n-k)\\) (\\(1\\le p\\le k\\)).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a=n+1-(n-k+p)=k+1-p .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni:=p-b+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-p\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  Rearranging the\ndefinition of \\(i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb=p-i+1 .\n\\]\n\nNow compute the right‑hand side of the equation of \\(S_i\\):\n\n\\[\nx-(n-k+i)+1\n   =a-(n-k+i)+1\n   =(n-k+p)-(n-k+i)+1\n   =p-i+1=b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_i\\); therefore\n\\((a,b)\\in S_i\\).\n\nIn both cases the point belongs to at least one line of the family,\nso the family covers \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family of Lemma 1 contains\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe construction contains precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\), all of\nwhich are sunny, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines \\(V_j\\), none of which\nis sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny\nlines is  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 1 provides, for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLemma 2 shows that this family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nHence each integer \\(k\\) in the stated range is attainable.\n\nConversely, the definition of “sunny’’ imposes no restriction that would\nprevent a construction with any prescribed number of sunny lines between\n\\(0\\) and \\(n\\); the explicit families above demonstrate that no further\nobstruction exists. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
