red by two sunny lines.\n\nThe lemma settles the original problem, because together with the trivial\nconstructions for \\(k=0,1\\) it yields a configuration for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\), and the impossibility for \\(k=2\\) follows from\nthe second part of the lemma.\n\nThe remainder of the solution is devoted to the proof of the lemma.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lemma \u2013 the case \\(m=2\\) (impossibility)\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\nA sunny line cannot be horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)),\nnor have slope \\(-1\\).  Any two distinct points of \\(T_2\\) determine a line\nwith slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain at\nmost one point of \\(T_2\\).  With only two sunny lines we can cover at most two\npoints, leaving the third uncovered.  Hence \\(T_2\\) cannot be covered by two\nsunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lemma \u2013 construction for \\(m\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(m\\ge 3\\).  We exhibit \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) that cover\n\\(T_m\\).\n\n*Lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\).*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + 1 - i .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(1\\), which is sunny.  Equation (4.1) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\na-b = i-1 .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the points of \\(T_m\\) whose difference\n\\(a-b\\) equals \\(i-1\\).  Since \\(0\\le a-b\\le m-3\\) for all points of\n\\(T_m\\) with \\(a\\ge b\\), the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-2}\\) cover **all**\npoints of \\(T_m\\) satisfying \\(a\\ge b\\).\n\n*Two additional lines for the points with \\(b>a\\).*  \nThe remaining points of \\(T_m\\) are those with \\(b>a\\).  They are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in T_m\\mid b>a\\bigr\\}\n   =\\{(a,m+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le \\lfloor\\tfrac{m}{2}\\rfloor\\}\n     \\cup\n     \\{(a,m-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le \\lfloor\\tfrac{m-1}{2}\\rfloor\\}.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nThese points lie on t