L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-2}\\) cover **all**\npoints of \\(T_m\\) satisfying \\(a\\ge b\\).\n\n*Two additional lines for the points with \\(b>a\\).*  \nThe remaining points of \\(T_m\\) are those with \\(b>a\\).  They are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in T_m\\mid b>a\\bigr\\}\n   =\\{(a,m+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le \\lfloor\\tfrac{m}{2}\\rfloor\\}\n     \\cup\n     \\{(a,m-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le \\lfloor\\tfrac{m-1}{2}\\rfloor\\}.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nThese points lie on the two lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{m-1}&:\\; y = -(m-2)\\,x + (2m-1),\\\\[2mm]\nL_{m}  &:\\; y = -\\frac{m-2}{\\,m-1\\,}\\,x + \\frac{m^{2}-m-1}{\\,m-1\\,}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L_{m-1}\\) and \\(L_m\\) are\nsunny.  A direct substitution shows that each point listed in (4.3) satisfies\nexactly one of the equations (4.4); consequently the two lines together cover\nall points of \\(T_m\\) with \\(b>a\\).\n\n*Verification that the \\(m\\) lines are distinct.*  \nThe slopes of the first \\(m-2\\) lines are all equal to \\(1\\), but their\nintercepts \\(1-i\\) are distinct, so the lines are distinct.  The slopes of\n\\(L_{m-1}\\) and \\(L_m\\) are \\(-(m-2)\\) and \\(-\\dfrac{m-2}{m-1}\\), respectively,\nwhich are different from each other and from \\(1\\).  Hence the whole family\n\\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\) consists of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_m\\) either satisfies \\(a\\ge b\\) and is therefore on one of\nthe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-2}\\) by (4.2), or satisfies \\(b>a\\) and is on\n\\(L_{m-1}\\) or \\(L_m\\) by (4.4).  Thus the union of the \\(m\\) lines contains\nall points of \\(T_m\\).\n\nThis completes the construction for all \\(m\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Completion of the original problem\n\n*Existence.*\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n* \\(k=1\\):  take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the sunny\n  line \\(y=x\\); the uncovered point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the latter line.  \n* \\(k\\ge 3\\):  use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\); the\n  remaining points form \\(T_k\\), which is covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n  supplied by the construction of §4.\n\n*Non‑existence for \\(k=2\\).*  \nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines existed.\nAfter removing the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines (which we may replace by vertical\nlines as in §2) the uncovered set is a translate of \\(T_2\\).  By the\nimpossibility proved in §3, two sunny lines cannot cover this translate,\na contradiction.  Hence no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists for any\n\\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\qquad\\text{(i.e. every integer }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ except }k=2\\text{).}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
