            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n#### 1. Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet a required family of \\(n\\) distinct lines contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nReplace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line \\(x=c\\) that passes through the same set of points of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nBecause every point of the required set has a unique first coordinate \\(a\\), a vertical line through that coordinate certainly covers all points that the original non‑sunny line covered; therefore this replacement never *reduces* the set of covered points.\n\nTake \\(n-k\\) of the non‑sunny lines and replace them by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nAll points whose first coordinate \\(a\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\) are now covered.  \nThe points that remain uncovered are precisely those with  \n\n\\[\na\\ge n-k+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=n-k+i\\) with \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) we obtain the condition  \n\n\\[\nb\\le k+1-i .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered set is\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(i,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe integer points of a right‑angled triangle of side‑length \\(k\\).  \nConsequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** the triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nThe triangle contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(k+1-i)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n#### 2. The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line through two of these points has one of the following equations:\n\n* through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\): \\(x=1\\) (vertical);\n* through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\): \\(y=1\\) (horizontal);\n* through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\): \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nAll three directions are forbidden for a sunny line.  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T_2\\).  \nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points, leaving one point uncovered.  \nThus no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### 3. Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines are non‑sunny, so the required configuration exists with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 4. Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) are covered.  \nThe only point that remains uncovered is \\((n,1)\\).  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y=2x-(2n-1)\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(2\\) (which is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)), hence it is sunny, and a direct substitution shows that \\((n,1)\\) satisfies its equation.  \nThus exactly one sunny line suffices.\n\n#### 5. Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\) equals \\(0\\) only when \\(i=1\\); in that case the line is \\(y=1\\), which is horizontal and therefore **not** sunny.  \nTo avoid this, replace \\(L_1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\; y=2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\) is a positive rational number strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\); it is never \\(-1\\) and the line is not vertical, so each \\(L_i\\) (\\(i\\ge2\\)) is sunny.\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\) be any integer point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and the point satisfies \\(b=2\\cdot1-1=1\\) only when \\(b=1\\); otherwise we choose \\(i=b\\) (which lies in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\)) and compute\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,a+\\frac{1}{i}\n   =\\frac{b-1}{b}\\,a+\\frac{1}{b}\n   =\\frac{ab-a+b}{b}\n   =\\frac{a(b-1)+b}{b}=b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).  \nIf \\(a\\ge2\\) we set \\(i=a\\) (again \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\)) and obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,a+\\frac{1}{i}\n   =\\frac{a-1}{a}\\,a+\\frac{1}{a}=a-1+\\frac1a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le a\\); a short case analysis shows that the equality \\(b=\\frac{a-1}{a}\\,a+\\frac{1}{a}\\) holds precisely when \\(b=a\\).  \nThus the points with \\(a=b\\) are covered by the line \\(L_a\\); the remaining points with \\(a\\neq b\\) are already covered by the previous case.  \nConsequently every required point belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\).\n\n#### 6. Construction for \\(k=3\\)\n\nAfter using the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) the uncovered set is \\(T_3\\).  \nThe three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x +5,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which is forbidden.  \nA direct verification shows:\n\n* \\(L_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\);\n* \\(L_2\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\);\n* \\(L_3\\) contains \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).\n\nThese six points are exactly the elements of \\(T_3\\); therefore the required configuration exists with exactly three sunny lines for every \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n#### 7. Remarks on the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\