n coverings for \\(T_4,T_5,\\dots ,T_n\\) by repeated application of the inductive step.  \n   Therefore for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet a required family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nEvery non‑sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nReplace each non‑sunny line by a **vertical** line that passes through the same set of points of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Why is this replacement legitimate?*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\) lies on a non‑sunny line, then its first coordinate is some integer \\(a\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) certainly contains \\((a,b)\\); therefore after the replacement no required point is lost.  \nThe replacement does not increase the number of sunny lines, because vertical lines are non‑sunny.\n\nConsequently we may assume that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points whose first coordinate belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) are now covered.  \nThe points that remain uncovered satisfy  \n\n\\[\na\\ge n-k+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=n-k+i\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k+1-i\\).  Hence the uncovered set is\n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+j\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_k\\) is the integer lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(k,1),(1,k)\\).  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(k+1-i)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** the triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by\n\\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  From now on we work exclusively with the covering problem for \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line that contains two of these three points has one of the following equations:\n\n* through \\((1,1),(1,2)\\): \\(x=1\\) (vertical);\n* through \\((1,1),(2,1)\\): \\(y=1\\) (horizontal);\n* through \\((1,2),(2,1)\\): \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nAll three directions are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T_2\\).  \nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points, leaving one point uncovered.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=2\\text{ is impossible for every }n\\ge3 } .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Explicit coverings for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every integer \\(k\\ge0,\\;k\\neq2\\), a family of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that covers \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The trivial cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  no sunny line is needed; the vertical lines (1) already cover all points, so the required configuration exists with \\(k=0\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\):  use the vertical lines (1) with \\(n-1\\) of them, i.e. \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only uncovered point of the whole set is \\((n,1)\\).  \n  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\; y=2x-(2n-1)\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n  has slope \\(2\\) (sunny) and passes through \\((n,1)\\).  Adding \\(L\\) gives a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  The case \\(k=3\\)\n\nThe triangle \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x +5,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which is forbidden.  Direct substitution shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1),(2,2) & L_1\\\\\n(1,2),(3,1) & L_2\\\\\n(1,3),(2,1) & L_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nso the six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered.  Hence a covering exists for \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  An inductive construction for all \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nWe prove the following statement by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n> **Induction Claim.**  \n> For every integer \\(k\\ge3\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover the triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe claim is true for \\(k=3\\) by the construction (6).  \nAssume it holds for some \\(k\\ge3\\); we construct a covering for \\(k+1\\).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) be a covering of \\(T_k\\).  \nThe triangle \\(T_{k+1}\\) is obtained from \\(T_k\\) by adding the **new outer layer**\n\n\\[\nU_{k+1}:=\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+j=k+2\\},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of the \\(k+1\\) points \\((1,k+1),(2,k),\\dots ,(k+1,1)\\).\n\nWe shall modify at most two of the old lines and add one new sunny line so that all points of \\(U_{k+1}\\) become covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3.1  Choice of the new line\n\nPick the point \\(P:=(k+1,1)\\in U_{k+1}\\).  \nDefine the new line  \n\n\\[\nL_{new}:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{k}\\,x + \\frac{k+1}{k}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(-\\frac1k\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (because \\(k\\ge3\\)), so \\(L_{new}\\) is sunny.  \nA direct computation shows that \\(L_{new}\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP=(k+1,1),\\qquad Q=(1,k+1),\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(U_{k+1}\\).  Thus \\(L_{new}\\) already covers two points of the new layer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3.2  Repairing the remaining points of the new layer\n\nThe remaining points of \\(U_{k+1}\\) are  \n\n\\[\nR_i:=(i,k+2-i),\\qquad i=2,3,\\dots ,k .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(i\\) the line \\(L_i\\) of the old covering \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains the point \\((i,k+1-i)\\) (because \\((i,k+1-i)\\in T_k\\)).  \nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is some value \\(m_i\\) that is not forbidden.  \nConsider the line obtained from \\(L_i\\) by **shifting its intercept** so that it now passes through \\(R_i\\) instead of \\((i,k+1-i)\\).  \nExplicitly, if \\(L_i\\) has equation \\(y=m_i x+b_i\\), replace it by  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_i:\\; y=m_i x + \\bigl(b_i + (k+2-i)-(k+1-i)\\bigr)\n               = m_i x + (b_i+1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe new line \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) still has slope \\(m_i\\); therefore it remains sunny.  \nMoreover \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) now contains the point \\(R_i\\).  \n\nBecause we change the intercept by the same amount \\(+1\\) for every modified line, distinctness of the lines is preserved: if two original lines were distinct, their shifted versions are still distinct (their intercepts differ by the same amount).  \n\nThus after the following operations\n\n* keep all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) unchanged except the \\(k-1\\) lines \\(L_i\\) with \\(i=2,\\dots ,k\\);\n* replace each of those \\(k-1\\) lines by \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) as in (11);\n* add the new line \\(L_{new}\\) from (8),\n\nwe obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k+1}\\) consisting of exactly \\((k-1)+1+1 = k+1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3.3  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_{k+1}\\) covers \\(T_{k+1}\\)\n\n*All points of \\(T_k\\)* are still covered: each point that was on a line \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\) now lies on \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) because the shift of the intercept does not affect the set of points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate; the point \\((1,k)\\) (which belongs to \\(L_1\\)) is untouched.\n\n*All points of the new layer \\(U_{k+1}\\)* are covered:  \n  – the two endpoints \\((1,k+1)\\) and \\((k+1,1)\\) lie on \\(L_{new}\\);  \n  – each interior point \\(R_i\\) lies on \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) by construction.\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{k+1}\\) is a covering of \\(T_{k+1}\\) by \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3.4  Completion of the induction\n\nThe induction basis \\(k=3\\) holds, and the inductive step shows that if a covering exists for some \\(k\\ge3\\) then one exists for \\(k+1\\).  Consequently a covering exists for **every** integer \\(k\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final assembly for the original problem\n\nFor a given \\(n\\ge3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\) proceed as follows.\n\n1. Use the vertical lines (1) with indices \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\).  \n   They are non‑sunny and cover all points whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\).\n\n2. The remaining points are exactly the triangle \\(T_k\\).  \n   By Sections 3.1–3.3 we have a family of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that covers \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe union of the two families consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Answer\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the non‑negative integers different from 2 that do not exceed \\(n\\).  In compact form\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  No configuration exists for \\(k=2\\)."
