            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet a required family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nEvery non‑sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nReplace each non‑sunny line by a **vertical** line that passes through the same set of points of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Why is this replacement legitimate?*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\) lies on a non‑sunny line, then its first coordinate is some integer \\(a\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) certainly contains \\((a,b)\\); therefore after the replacement no required point is lost.  \nThe replacement does not increase the number of sunny lines, because vertical lines are non‑sunny.\n\nConsequently we may assume that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points whose first coordinate belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) are now covered.  \nThe points that remain uncovered satisfy  \n\n\\[\na\\ge n-k+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=n-k+i\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k+1-i\\).  Hence the uncovered set is\n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+j\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_k\\) is the integer lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(k,1),(1,k)\\).  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(k+1-i)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** the triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by\n\\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  From now on we work exclusively with the covering problem for \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line that contains two of these three points has one of the following equations:\n\n* through \\((1,1),(1,2)\\): \\(x=1\\) (vertical);\n* through \\((1,1),(2,1)\\): \\(y=1\\) (horizontal);\n* through \\((1,2),(2,1)\\): \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nAll three directions are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T_2\\).  \nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points, leaving one point uncovered.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=2\\text{ is impossible for every }n\\ge3 } .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Explicit coverings for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every integer \\(k\\ge0,\\;k\\neq2\\), a family of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that covers \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The trivial cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  no sunny line is needed; the vertical lines (1) already cover all points, so the required configuration exists with \\(k=0\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\):  use the vertical lines (1) with \\(n-1\\) of them, i.e. \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only uncovered point of the whole set is \\((n,1)\\).  \n  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\; y=2x-(2n-1)\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n  has slope \\(2\\) (sunny) and passes through \\((n