ings for \\(T_4,T_5,\\dots ,T_n\\) by repeated application of the inductive step.  \n   Therefore for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet a required family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nEvery non‑sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nReplace each non‑sunny line by a **vertical** line that passes through the same set of points of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Why is this replacement legitimate?*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\) lies on a non‑sunny line, then its first coordinate is some integer \\(a\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) certainly contains \\((a,b)\\); therefore after the replacement no required point is lost.  \nThe replacement does not increase the number of sunny lines, because vertical lines are non‑sunny.\n\nConsequently we may assume that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll points whose first coordinate belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) are now covered.  \nThe points that remain uncovered satisfy  \n\n\\[\na\\ge n-k+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=n-k+i\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k+1-i\\).  Hence the uncovered set is\n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+j\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_k\\) is the integer lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(k,1),(1,k)\\).  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(k+