ix points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x +5,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which is forbidden.  Direct substitution shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1),(2,2) & L_1\\\\\n(1,2),(3,1) & L_2\\\\\n(1,3),(2,1) & L_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nso the six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered.  Hence a covering exists for \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  An inductive construction for all \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nWe prove the following statement by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n> **Induction Claim.**  \n> For every integer \\(k\\ge3\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover the triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe claim is true for \\(k=3\\) by the construction (6).  \nAssume it holds for some \\(k\\ge3\\); we construct a covering for \\(k+1\\).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) be a covering of \\(T_k\\).  \nThe triangle \\(T_{k+1}\\) is obtained from \\(T_k\\) by adding the **new outer layer**\n\n\\[\nU_{k+1}:=\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+j=k+2\\},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of the \\(k+1\\) points \\((1,k+1),(2,k),\\dots ,(k+1,1)\\).\n\nWe shall modify at most two of the old lines and add one new sunny line so that all points of \\(U_{k+1}\\) become covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3.1  Choice of the new line\n\nPick the point \\(P:=(k+1,1)\\in U_{k+1}\\).  \nDefine the new line  \n\n\\[\nL_{new}:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{k}\\,x + \\frac{k+1}{k}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(-\\frac1k\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (because \\(k\\ge3\\)), so \\(L_{new}\\) is sunny.  \nA direct computation shows that \\(L_{new}\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP=(k+1,1),\\qquad Q=(1,k+1),\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(U_{k+1}\\).  Thus \\(L_{new}\\) already covers two points of the new layer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3.2  Repairing the remaining points of the new layer\n\nThe remaining points of \\(U_{k+1}\\) are  \n\n\\[\nR_i:=(i,k+2-i),\\qquad i=2,3,\\dots ,k .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(i\\) the line \\(L_i\\) of the old covering \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains the point \\((i,k+1-i)\\) (because \\((i,k+1-i)\\in T_k\\)).  \nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is some value \\(m_i\\) that is not forbidden.  \nConsider the line obtained from \\(L_i\\) by **shifting its intercept** so that it now passes through \\(R_i\\) instead of \\((i,k+1-i)\\).  \nExplicitly, if \\(L_i\\) has equation \\(y=m_i x+b_i\\), replace it by  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_i:\\; y=m_i x + \\bigl(b_i + (k+2-i)-(k+1-i)\\bigr)\n               = m_i x + (b_i+1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe new line \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) still has slope \\(m_i\\); therefore it remains sunny.  \nMoreover \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) now contains the point \\(R_i\\).  \n\nBecause we change the intercept by the same amount \\(+1\\) for every modified line, distinctness of the lines is preserved: if two original lines were distinct, their shifted versions are still distinct (their intercepts differ by the same amount).  \n\nThus after the following operations\n\n* keep all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) unchanged except the \\(k-1\\) lines \\(L_i\\) with \\(i=2,\\dots ,k\\);\n* replace each of those \\(k-1\\) lines by \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) as in (11);\n* add the new line \\(L_{new}\\) from (8),\n\nwe obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k+1}\\) consisting of exactly \\((k-1)+1+1 = k+1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3.3  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_{k+1}\\) covers \\(T_{k+1}\\)\n\n*All points of \\(T_k\\)* are still covered: each point that was on a line \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\) now lies on \\(\\