each sunny line covers at most  \n\n\\[\nM(k):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_{k}\\).  With \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most\n\\(kM(k)\\) points.  Comparing with (11) we obtain the necessary inequality\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, write \\(k=2m\\); then \\(M(k)=m\\) and the left\u2013hand side of\n(13) equals \\(m(2m+1)\\), which is strictly larger than \\(2m\\cdot m=2m^{2}\\).\nThus (13) fails for every even \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd, write \\(k=2m+1\\); then \\(M(k)=m+1\\) and both sides of (13)\nequal \\((2m+1)(m+1)\\).  Consequently (13) holds exactly for\n\n\\[\nk=0,\\;k=1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nTherefore (14) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of the required\nfamily of lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A covering of \\(T_{k}\\) for odd \\(k\\)\n\nWe now prove that condition (14) is also **sufficient** by constructing, for every\nodd \\(k\\), a family of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that covers \\(T_{k}\\).\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;L_{r}:\\; y = -\\frac{r}{m}\\,x + \\bigl(m+1\\bigr) + \\frac{r}{m}\\qquad\n      (r=0,1,\\dots ,2m,\\;r\\neq m)\\;}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nand additionally  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;L_{2m}:\\; y = -2x + (2m+3).\\;}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n*The slopes.*  \nFor \\(r\\neq m\\) the slope \\(-\\dfrac{r}{m}\\) is a rational number different from\n\\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); for \\(r=2m\\) the slope is \\(-2\\).  Hence every line\nlisted in (15)\u2013(16) is sunny, and all the lines are distinct because their\nslopes are distinct.\n\n*Points of \\(T_{k}\\) on the lines.*  \nFix \\(r\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,2m\\}\\setminus\\{m\\}\\).  Solving the equation of \\(L_{r}\\)\nfor integer points gives  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{r}{m}x + m+1+\\frac{r}{m}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nmx + ry = m(m+1)+r .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gcd(m,r)=1\\) (otherwise we could cancel a common factor and obtain a\ndifferent line already present in the family), the integer solutions of (17)\nwith \\(x,y>0\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(i,\\;m+1-\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{ri}{m}\\bigr\\rfloor\\bigr),\n\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,m+1 .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nFor each admissible \\(i\\) the second coordinate is positive and\n\\(i+(m+1-\\lfloor ri/m\\rfloor)\\le 2m+2=k+1\\); therefore the points in (18) lie\nin \\(T_{k}\\).  Because the map \\(i\\mapsto\\bigl\\lfloor ri/m\\rfloor\\) is strictly\nmonotone, the \\(m+1\\) points in (18) are distinct.  Consequently **every line\n\\(L_{r}\\) with \\(r\\neq m\\) contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{k}\\).**\n\nFor the special line \\(L_{2m}\\) (16) we have  \n\n\\[\ny = -2x + (2m+3) .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nThe integer points of \\(T_{k}\\) satisfying (19) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,2m+1),\\;(2,2m-1),\\dots ,\\;(m+1,m+1),\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nagain precisely \\(m+1\\) points, all belonging to \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n*Covering.*  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{L_{r}\\mid r=0,1,\\dots ,2m,\\;r\\neq m\\}\\cup\\{L_{2m}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(2m+1=k\\) distinct sunny lines.  By the discussion above each line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contributes exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{k}\\), and the total\nnumber of points contributed is  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot(m+1)= (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_{k}|.\n\\]\n\nHence the points contributed by the lines are precisely all points of \\(T_{k}\\);\nno point is missed and no point is counted twice.  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) is a\ncovering of \\(T_{k}\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction works for every odd \\(k\\ge3\\); the cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are\ntrivial (no sunny line, or one sunny line together with the vertical lines\n(9)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the original problem\n\nGiven \\(n\\ge3\\) and a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) satisfying (14),\n\n1. take the vertical non\u2011sunny lines (9); they are \\(n-k\\) distinct lines;\n2. cover the remaining triangle \\(T_{k}\\) with the sunny lines \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)\n   constructed in \u00a73.\n\nThe union consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nConversely, if a configuration exists, the counting argument of \u00a72 forces\n\\(k\\) to satisfy (14).  Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\nthose described in (14).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid k\\text{ odd},\\;3\\le k\\le n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. all non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) except the single value\n\\(k=2\\).  No configuration exists for \\(k=2\\), and for every other admissible\n\\(k\\) a configuration can be built as described above."

