            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a triangle\n\nFix a non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\).  \nChoose the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct, non‑sunny and together contain every point of\n\\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  \nThe points of \\(S_n\\) that are not covered are precisely\n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nthe integer points of a right‑angled triangle of side‑length \\(k\\).  Its\ncardinality is\n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{x=1}^{k}(k+1-x)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nConsequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A bound for a sunny line inside \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line that contains at least two integer points of\n\\(T_k\\).  Between any two consecutive integer points on \\(L\\) the vector\ndifference is \\((q,p)\\) where \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\), \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and\n\\(p/q\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\).\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\) is an integer point of \\(L\\), then moving one step along the\ndirection \\((q,p)\\) changes the sum \\(x+y\\) by \\(|p|+|q|\\).  All points of\n\\(T_k\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\); therefore the number of integer points\nof \\(L\\) that lie in \\(T_k\\) is at most\n\n\\[\n1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-2}{|p|+|q|}\\Big\\rfloor\\le\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThus every sunny line meets \\(T_k\\) in at most  \n\n\\[\nM(k):=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nSince the \\(k\\) sunny lines must cover all \\(|T_k|=k(k+1)/2\\) points, (16)\ngives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\,M(k).\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, write \\(k=2m\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Then \\(M(k)=m\\) and (17) becomes\n\\(m(2m+1)\\le 2m^{2}\\), which is false.  Hence no even \\(k\\ge2\\) is possible.\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd, write \\(k=2m+1\\).  Then \\(M(k)=m+1\\) and (17) holds with\nequality.  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are trivially realizable (all lines\nvertical, or all but one vertical).  Therefore a **necessary** condition is  \n\n\\[\nk=0,\\;k=1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd}.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nAssume \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  Define the following \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*For each integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le 2m\\) and \\(r\\neq m\\):*\n\n\\[\nL_r:\\; y=-\\frac{r}{m}\\,x+\\Bigl(m+1+\\frac{r}{m}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-r/m\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus each \\(L_r\\) is\nsunny.\n\n*Two additional lines:*\n\n\\[\nL_{0}:\\; y=x ,\\qquad\nL_{m}:\\; y=-2x+(2m+3).\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nBoth are sunny (slopes \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\)).  Altogether we have exactly\n\\(k=2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1 Points of \\(T_k\\) lying on the lines\n\nFor a fixed \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,2m\\}\\setminus\\{m\\}\\) multiply the equation of\n\\(L_r\\) by \\(m\\); we obtain  \n\n\\[\nmx+ry=m(m+1)+r .\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd(m,r)=1\\), the integer solutions with \\(x>0\\) are obtained by\nchoosing \\(x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le m+1)\\) and setting  \n\n\\[\ny=m+1-\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{ri}{m}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nHence the points of \\(T_k\\) on \\(L_r\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nP_{r,i}=\\bigl(i,\\;m+1-\\big\\lfloor\\tfrac{ri}{m}\\big\\rfloor\\bigr),\n\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,m+1 .\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nEach such point satisfies \\(i+(m+1-\\lfloor ri/m\\rfloor)\\le 2m+2\\); therefore\n\\(P_{r,i}\\in T_k\\).  The \\(m+1\\) points are distinct because the map\n\\(i\\mapsto\\lfloor ri/m\\rfloor\\) is strictly monotone for the admissible\nrange of \\(r\\).\n\nFor the line \\(L_{0}\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nP_{0,i}=(i,i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,m+1 ,\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nand for \\(L_{m}\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nP_{m,i}=(i,\\,2m+3-2i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,m+1 .\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nAll points in (23)–(25) belong to \\(T_k\\), and each line contains exactly\n\\(m+1\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2 Disjointness of the point sets\n\nFix an index \\(i\\).  For \\(0\\le r\\le m\\) the quantity\n\\(m+1-\\lfloor ri/m\\rfloor\\) is strictly decreasing in \\(r\\); for\n\\(m\\le r\\le 2m\\) it is strictly increasing.  Consequently for a given\n\\(i\\) the second coordinates in (23) are all different when \\(r\\) varies.\nThe second coordinates in (24) equal \\(i\\) and those in (25) equal\n\\(2m+3-2i\\); neither of these values can coincide with any\n\\(m+1-\\lfloor ri/m\\rfloor\\) because the latter is never equal to \\(i\\) nor\nto \\(2m+3-2i\\) for the admissible \\(r\\).  Hence the \\(k\\) point‑sets are\npairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3 Covering of \\(T_k\\)\n\nEach of the \\(k\\) lines contributes exactly \\(m+1\\) points, so the total\nnumber of covered points is  \n\n\\[\nk\\,(m+1)=(2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_k|.\n\\]\n\nSince all covered points lie in \\(T_k\\) and their number equals \\(|T_k|\\),\nthey must be precisely the whole set \\(T_k\\).  Thus the family\n\\(\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{2m}\\}\\) covers \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Completion of the original problem\n\nFor any \\(n\\ge3\\) and any \\(k\\) satisfying (18) we:\n\n1. take the vertical non‑sunny lines (12);\n2. cover the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\) with the sunny lines constructed in\n   §4 (when \\(k\\) is odd) or with a single sunny line (when \\(k=1\\));\n3. obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\n   and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nConversely, the counting argument of §3 shows that no other values of \\(k\\)\nare possible.  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{or}\\;k\\text{ odd with }3\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the proof.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
